        <div id="vs-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Egg White Protein vs Whey: Leucine, Speed and Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            The question most buyers have is whether egg white protein builds muscle as well as whey. The honest answer is that it is close, slightly behind on paper, and the gap is easy to close. Both are complete, highly digestible animal proteins. The measurable difference is leucine, the essential amino acid that acts as the main trigger for muscle protein synthesis after a meal or a workout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whey is unusually rich in leucine, at roughly 10 to 11 percent of its protein depending on the analysis. Egg protein typically comes in a couple of points lower, around 8 to 9 percent; one controlled feeding study in rats used meals with leucine at 10.9 percent for whey and 8.8 percent for egg. In practice, 25 g of whey supplies something like 2.5 to 2.7 g of leucine, while 25 g of egg white protein lands closer to 2.1 to 2.2 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap matters less than marketing suggests. In one of the best-known dose studies, published in 2009, researchers fed young men 0, 5, 10, 20 or 40 g of whole egg protein after leg training and found muscle protein synthesis was maximally stimulated at 20 g, with no significant extra benefit from 40 g. Egg protein was the test protein precisely because it is such a reliable, high-quality source. If you want to match whey's leucine, simply take a slightly larger serving, around 30 g of egg protein instead of 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digestion speed is the other difference. Whey is absorbed very quickly; egg white protein is generally described as moderate, slower than whey but faster than casein. Over a full day of eating, the total protein you consume and how consistently you train matter far more than which of these two you drink. Our guide to <a href="/highest-quality-protein/">the highest quality protein sources</a> explains how scores such as PDCAAS and DIAAS are worked out if you want the detail.
          </p>

