        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming egg is safe for an egg allergy because it is just the white</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The white is where most of the egg allergens live, so egg white powder is concentrated allergen for anyone with an egg allergy. If you have reacted to eggs before, avoid these products completely and ask an allergist or registered dietitian to help you find a safe dairy-free protein instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a blend when you need dairy-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some brands sell egg alongside whey and casein in blended powders with similar packaging. Naked Nutrition, for example, sells both a pure egg white powder and a whey, casein and egg blend. Read the ingredient list and allergen statement every time, not just the front label, before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing all the flour with egg protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Egg protein sets firm when heated, so swapping it for all the flour in a recipe gives rubbery, dry results. Start by replacing a quarter to a third of the flour, keep fat and moisture in the recipe, and check for doneness a minute or two early.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sodium</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 25 g protein serving of egg white powder often carries 300 to 400 mg of sodium, noticeably more than most whey or pea powders. That is fine for most people, but if you follow a sodium limit, count it like any other food and ask your clinician what suits you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying the egg premium when whey would do</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If your only issue with dairy is lactose, a whey isolate is very low in lactose, carries more leucine and usually costs less per 25 g of protein. Try an isolate first; egg is best reserved for people with a milk allergy or a real reason to avoid dairy.
              </p>
